To inspect the structure without running a Windows process, use Clang and LLD with the Windows MSVC target. Save this definition as \texttt{arith.c}:

\begin{verbatim}
__declspec(dllexport) int twice(int x) { return 2*x; }
\end{verbatim}

Save the caller as \texttt{client.c}:

\begin{verbatim}
__declspec(dllimport) int twice(int);
int main(void) { return twice(21) != 42; }
\end{verbatim}

\begin{verbatim}
clang --target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc -ffreestanding \
    -fno-stack-protector -c arith.c client.c
lld-link /dll /noentry /nodefaultlib /out:arith.dll \
    /implib:arith.lib arith.o
lld-link /entry:main /subsystem:console /nodefaultlib \
    /out:client.exe client.o arith.lib
llvm-readobj --coff-exports arith.dll
llvm-readobj --coff-imports client.exe
\end{verbatim}

The local cross-link yields ordinal \(1\) at RVA \texttt{0x1000} and a named import of \texttt{twice} from \texttt{arith.dll}; the reported hint is \(0\). The RVAs are observations of one build, not promised addresses.

\section{Binding Is Not Rebasing or Delay Loading}

COFF relocations are consumed when the linker places a symbol such as \verb|__imp_twice| in the consumer's image. PE base relocations are a separate mechanism: they adjust selected addresses when an image moves from its preferred base. They do not find \texttt{twice} in a DLL. Ordinary import binding populates that consumer's IAT before the client makes its first call. Toolchain-supported delay imports instead use a helper that can defer loading the DLL as well as binding the procedure. This differs from the classic ELF lazy PLT for a startup dependency, where the library is ordinarily already mapped and only the lookup for that call is deferred.

PE also has a \emph{bound import} representation for saving previously computed import addresses. Such addresses are useful only when the loader can establish that the provider matches and the resulting addresses apply to this mapping. Bound imports are neither the normal symbolic import description nor a request for delay loading. The three cases differ in when the target address is computed and what information is still needed at load time.
